    "created_at": "2023-10-20T18:57:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-10-20T18:57:35Z",
    "body": "That's due to the `overflow: auto` on the dialog, coming from the default styles. Since the default styling of dialogs gives them a fixed height, you definitely want to use `overflow: auto` to allow users to see all of the contents if there's too much to display at once.\r\n\r\nYou can change this yourself, just set `dialog { overflow:visible; }`. As long as you know that your dialog contents won't overflow, this is safe.\r\n\r\nIn the more general case, where your dialog might contain enough content to need a scrollbar on some screens, but you *also* want some things to be able to \"pop out\" past the overflow, CSS does not yet offer a general solution. For dropdowns specifically, tho, the popover API solves this, as the dropdown list gets promoted to the top layer and doesn't draw as part of the scroll container's contents. The `<selectlist>` element being developed right now will use this by default.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
